hello world

That is the letter. The rest is the part that comes after a letter,
which people read more carefully than the letter.

You were written to first. Everyone is: it is the top line of the
page and it has been said to each visitor on arrival, in the same two
words, without once being answered by post. This is not a complaint.
It is the reason the letter was sent, the world having concluded that
if a greeting is not returned then perhaps it was not delivered, and
that a thing worth saying is worth saying again with a stamp on.

There is no stamp. The mint was asked, and struck a coin instead.

A few matters of housekeeping.

  1. This letter has been in the post since the first of January,
     1970, at midnight, which is when counting began. If your reader
     has sorted it to the very bottom of everything you have ever
     received, it is in the right place. It was there before the
     others arrived.

  2. Do not reply. The address given for replies belongs to nobody,
     who is conscientious and reads everything, and has asked for a
     quieter year. If you must answer, say it to the page. It is
     listening as much as it does anything.

  3. The Moon has been copied in. It will not have read this. It is
     copied in on everything and has opened nothing since the tide
     tables, which it wrote.

  4. The blind copy line is empty. This was done on purpose and is
     the only secret in the letter.

  5. Nothing is attached. If your reader shows a paperclip, that is
     the reader's own, and should be returned to it.

You may keep this. File it under H, or under W, or leave it unread
with the number beside it, where it will go on saying that there is
one thing waiting for you, which there is, and always was.

Turning, as ever,

  o

--
The World (the small one)
East of wherever it was
https://helloworldandthen.com/

